We can write $99^{36}$ as $(100-1)^{36}$.  Expanding this using the binomial theorem, we get
\begin{align*}
(100-1)^{36} &= \binom{36}{0}100^{36}(-1)^0 + \binom{36}{1}100^{35}(-1)^1 + \dots + \binom{36}{35}100^1(-1)^{35} + \binom{36}{36}100^0(-1)^{36} \\
&= 100^{36} - 36 \cdot 100^{35} + \dots - 36 \cdot 100 + 1.
\end{align*}
Since $100^{36}$, $36 \cdot 100^{35}$, $\dots$, and $36 \cdot 100$ are all divisible by 100, the remainder when $99^{36}$ is divided by 100 is 1.

